Subject: the crosshair gets the depth of what it points at (D-064)

the user: "why can't the crosshair just have dynamic depth? like we do in the
quakes. instead of a fixed distance. it adjusts depending on where it points?"

THE PROBLEM. The crosshair is a 2D rectangle at a screen position PD computed
itself (sight.c, a dozen gDPHudRectangle calls), so the stereo fold never sees
it and both eyes draw it in the same pixels: zero disparity, ON the panel, at
the convergence plane, whatever it is pointing at. Aim at a wall a metre away
and the reticle is eight metres behind that wall; aim down a corridor and it is
in front of the far end. The only way to LOOK at it is to un-fuse whatever it
is sitting on, which is what "fuses at no distance" means in the family's own
notes (quake3e: "A crosshair on a flat overlay sits at the near plane in both
eyes and fuses at no distance"; q2repro L-25: "A flat crosshair fuses at no
distance").

THE FAMILY'S ANSWER, followed here. q2repro's CL_VR_AddAimDot and quake3e's aim
marker both take an ENGINE-SIDE COLLISION TRACE along the aim ray, every frame,
and place the marker at the hit point - a client trace against the collision
model, never a GPU depth readback (which is not even available to us: ES 3.0
does not accept GL_DEPTH_COMPONENT in glReadPixels). PD has the same trace:
cdExamLos08 + cdGetPos is how player.c pulls the third-person camera in past a
wall and how chraction.c places a bullet hole. And it has the same
screen-to-view-ray helper the sky uses, cam0f0b4c3c, whose vector has a view z
of exactly -arg2 - so the hit's FRACTION along the ray times arg2 is the VIEW
DEPTH, which is the number the fold wants, for a centred reticle and for the
off-centre one PD's aim mode produces alike. One LOS trace per frame.

Sky, or no hit at all: SIGHT_RETICLE_FAR (4000 units), which the fold turns
into the far asymptote - the sky's own disparity, where it belongs.

SMOOTHING, which the siblings do NOT do. Theirs is world GEOMETRY: it is at the
hit point because it is drawn there, so it cannot jitter. Ours is a flat
rectangle being SHIFTED by a disparity we compute, and a disparity that steps
when the aim sweeps past a doorway is a reticle that jumps. So it is smoothed
with a 100 ms time constant, frame-rate independent off g_Vars.lvupdate60, and
smoothed in 1/d rather than d - which is the space the disparity is LINEAR in,
so the ease is even across a near edge and a far one instead of crawling across
one and snapping across the other.

THE NEAR CLAMP APPLIES TO IT. The shift runs through the same clamped disparity
law as the world (D-061, gfx_draw_rectangle's depth-tag path), so the reticle
cannot land in front of the viewmodel's plane: it sits on the surface it points
at until that surface reaches the clamp, and then it stops with it.

THE MECHANISM is patch 0033's light-glare tag, made STICKY. PD_DLTAG_DEPTH is
consumed by ONE rectangle, which is right for a glare (artifact.c draws one per
light) and wrong for a crosshair (sight.c draws a dozen), so a new span pair
PD_DLSPAN_DEPTH_BEGIN/END holds the tag across all of them. One trace, one tag,
every piece of the reticle.

Everywhere but visionOS the trace and the tags are removed by the preprocessor
- the trace by TARGET_OS_VISION, and there is no cost anywhere else, not even
the G_NOOP.

ONE MORE THING, WHICH COST A GATE RUN.  This file is compiled into every
visionOS build, 2D included, and in 2D none of it may happen: cdExamLos08
touches the collision module's own static state, so a trace running under a 2D
--fixed-step replay is a determinism input the macOS oracle does not have, and
the M-001 gate is exactly the assertion that no such thing exists.  The first
run of it said "the replay never reached frame 2000".  TARGET_OS_VISION is a
BUILD, not a MODE: the trace and both tags are behind a runtime
pdVisionStereoIsActive() as well, so in 2D the display list is byte-identical
to the one the oracle walks.
